  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "Travel Editions",
    "overview": "Travel Editions treats each trip as a journal the traveler can edit. Photography establishes the destination on the dashboard. Inside the planner, the date, stop names, and numbered map markers carry the identity. A traveler should be able to find Point State Park in the itinerary, select it, and recognize the same stop on the map.\n\nThe dashboard has one prominent creation action and a searchable shelf of saved trips. The planner supports denser work: ordering stops, moving them between days, editing notes, and calculating routes. Both use the same paper and ink surfaces, serif headings, and burgundy selection treatment.",
    "dos": [
      "Do use real destinations, trip names, dates, and stop counts to establish hierarchy.",
      "Do maintain the selected stop across the list-to-map jump and return.",
      "Do retain photo fallback, provider credit, and routing limitations when compacting a surface."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't present an editable sample as a saved trip before the traveler adds it.",
